1
00:00:02,462 --> 00:00:05,381
Look at him. Breaks my heart.

2
00:00:05,590 --> 00:00:07,675
Poor little guy, all alone.

3
00:00:08,801 --> 00:00:11,929
I don't see why his brother can't sit with him. Come on, Mary.

4
00:00:12,138 --> 00:00:15,029
When you were in high school, would you have lunch with a nine-year-old? Yes, I would've.

5
00:00:15,141 --> 00:00:16,661
Well, there's something wrong with you.

6
00:00:17,268 --> 00:00:18,352
I'm gonna keep him company.

7
00:00:18,561 --> 00:00:19,812
Hey, hey, hey, Mary. Mary.

8
00:00:20,021 --> 00:00:23,441
Think this through. Right now the kids are just ignoring Sheldon.

9
00:00:23,649 --> 00:00:25,651
What happens if he's sitting with his mommy?

10
00:00:26,152 --> 00:00:27,904
They could mistake me for a senior.

11
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:31,199
Well, I look younger than you.

12
00:00:32,074 --> 00:00:33,218
Just give this time, all right?

13
00:00:33,242 --> 00:00:34,619
It'll work itself out.

14
00:00:36,537 --> 00:00:40,833
My mother never understood that I actually enjoyed being alone.

15
00:00:41,083 --> 00:00:48,883
Solitude allowed me to think about important things, like the effect of gravitational forces as you approach an event horizon,

16
00:00:50,426 --> 00:00:55,431
as opposed to less important things, like how many grapes my brother can fit in his mouth.

17
00:00:55,848 --> 00:00:56,641
14!

18
00:00:56,849 --> 00:00:58,476
Yeah!

19
00:00:59,352 --> 00:01:03,314
Jean-Paul sartre said, "hell is other people."

20
00:01:04,524 --> 00:01:07,026
That's humorous because it's true.

21
00:01:08,361 --> 00:01:11,239
♪ nobody else is stronger than I am

22
00:01:11,447 --> 00:01:14,075
♪ yesterday I moved a mountain

23
00:01:14,283 --> 00:01:16,661
♪ I bet I could be your hero

24
00:01:16,869 --> 00:01:19,580
♪ I am a mighty little man

25
00:01:19,789 --> 00:01:22,833
♪ I am a mighty little man.

26
00:01:32,885 --> 00:01:33,885
Something wrong?

27
00:01:34,470 --> 00:01:37,431
How come math is easy for Sheldon and hard for me?

28
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:40,268
I don't know, honey, but you have your own gifts.

29
00:01:40,601 --> 00:01:41,602
Like what?

30
00:01:41,811 --> 00:01:43,437
Well, like...

31
00:01:47,233 --> 00:01:49,110
You have very pretty hair.

32
00:01:50,027 --> 00:01:51,027
True.

33
00:01:53,447 --> 00:01:54,490
Georgie.

34
00:01:54,699 --> 00:01:56,450
Would you do me a little tiny favor?

35
00:01:56,659 --> 00:01:57,451
Like what?

36
00:01:57,660 --> 00:02:00,413
Tomorrow at lunch, would you mind sitting with your brother?

37
00:02:00,621 --> 00:02:01,621
Don't do it, Georgie.

38
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:03,124
You stay out of this.

39
00:02:03,416 --> 00:02:04,643
I ate with him in second grade.

40
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:06,669
It really hurt my social life.

41
00:02:06,919 --> 00:02:09,463
Sorry, mom. Can't do it.

42
00:02:11,799 --> 00:02:12,925
Why are you such a brat?

43
00:02:13,342 --> 00:02:15,261
Maybe I'm crying for attention.

44
00:02:16,512 --> 00:02:17,888
This isn't a joke, young lady.

45
00:02:18,389 --> 00:02:22,643
Yes, your brother may be smart, but he doesn't have a friend in the world.

46
00:02:22,852 --> 00:02:26,105
And I am sorry, but I am very worried about him.

47
00:02:30,901 --> 00:02:33,321
Nice going. You're making mom a nervous wreck.

48
00:02:33,988 --> 00:02:34,989
What did I do?

49
00:02:35,197 --> 00:02:37,867
You don't have any friends, and she is beside herself.

50
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:39,410
But I'm fine without friends.

51
00:02:39,619 --> 00:02:41,096
I don't know what to tell you, Sheldon.

52
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:43,039
She said it's your fault.

53
00:02:43,247 --> 00:02:45,166
I'm going to watch alf.

54
00:02:45,958 --> 00:02:50,338
Science fact: Sisters are the worst.

55
00:02:56,135 --> 00:03:03,476
When you're three foot ten and in high school, getting from point "a" to point "b" can be a harrowing experience.

56
00:03:03,684 --> 00:03:04,477
♪ I oh, yeah

57
00:03:04,685 --> 00:03:05,925
♪ I we are the youth gone wild

58
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:07,688
♪ whoa

59
00:03:07,897 --> 00:03:10,191
♪ whoa...

60
00:03:11,025 --> 00:03:14,028
adult Sheldon: But it's worth it when point "b" is...

61
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:17,948
The library.

62
00:03:18,324 --> 00:03:23,162
Home to the original information superhighway, the Dewey decimal system.

63
00:03:23,371 --> 00:03:24,651
Hey there, Sheldon.

64
00:03:24,872 --> 00:03:26,207
What can I get for you today?

65
00:03:26,916 --> 00:03:27,958
Chemistry?

66
00:03:28,167 --> 00:03:30,211
Quantum mechanics? Astronomy?

67
00:03:30,503 --> 00:03:31,879
No, nothing fun today.

68
00:03:32,088 --> 00:03:33,923
I need to learn how to make a friend.

69
00:03:34,131 --> 00:03:37,134
Aw, honey, you having a hard time adjusting to high school?

70
00:03:37,343 --> 00:03:39,762
I'm having a hard time adjusting to earth.

71
00:03:40,096 --> 00:03:42,056
Yeah. You and me both.

72
00:03:45,726 --> 00:03:46,519
Try this.

73
00:03:46,727 --> 00:03:49,438
It's been around a while, but it's still quite popular.

74
00:03:50,064 --> 00:03:53,317
Unlike me, who's just been around a while.

75
00:03:57,321 --> 00:04:00,032
"How to win friends and influence people."

76
00:04:00,408 --> 00:04:01,409
This might do it.

77
00:04:02,410 --> 00:04:03,744
"Principle one.

78
00:04:04,954 --> 00:04:07,123
Don't criticize, condemn or complain."

79
00:04:07,832 --> 00:04:11,001
The three sharpest arrows in my quiver.

80
00:04:11,752 --> 00:04:15,004
Thus began the greatest challenge of my young life.

81
00:04:15,172 --> 00:04:16,298
Hello, George.

82
00:04:17,466 --> 00:04:18,008
What?

83
00:04:18,217 --> 00:04:21,429
I do admire your thick head of hair...

84
00:04:21,721 --> 00:04:22,721
George.

85
00:04:22,763 --> 00:04:23,763
Thanks.

86
00:04:26,016 --> 00:04:26,809
What's going on?

87
00:04:27,017 --> 00:04:29,478
Well, I'm practicing the principles in this book.

88
00:04:30,062 --> 00:04:36,485
Why? Well, I know mom is concerned that I don't have any friends, so I'm determined to remedy the situation.

89
00:04:37,153 --> 00:04:38,153
0h.

90
00:04:38,529 --> 00:04:39,529
George.

91
00:04:39,739 --> 00:04:41,282
Well, good for you.

92
00:04:41,907 --> 00:04:44,660
Do you feel complimented when I say your name, George?

93
00:04:44,869 --> 00:04:47,413
The book says you should. George.

94
00:04:47,747 --> 00:04:48,914
Isuppose.

95
00:04:49,373 --> 00:04:50,573
Kind of overdoing it a little.

96
00:04:51,417 --> 00:04:54,003
Sheldon. Well, thank you for your criticism.

97
00:04:54,462 --> 00:04:57,882
It also says I should praise any improvements that you've made.

98
00:04:59,383 --> 00:05:00,426
Okay.

99
00:05:02,803 --> 00:05:04,054
I'll get back to you.

100
00:05:06,974 --> 00:05:08,100
George.

101
00:05:10,561 --> 00:05:17,234
On the heels of that successful beta test, I decided to practice the carnegie principles on someone my ow" age.

102
00:05:18,819 --> 00:05:19,445
♪ hey.

103
00:05:19,653 --> 00:05:22,448
hello, Billy sparks. Hey, Sheldon.

104
00:05:22,698 --> 00:05:27,787
I am genuinely interested in you and would like to encourage you to talk about yourself.

105
00:05:28,037 --> 00:05:29,037
Billy sparks.

106
00:05:29,079 --> 00:05:30,079
Thank you.

107
00:05:36,962 --> 00:05:38,339
You have the floor.

108
00:05:38,964 --> 00:05:40,216
Thank you.

109
00:05:43,093 --> 00:05:46,722
Looking back, I would've had better luck making friends with the chickens.

110
00:05:47,973 --> 00:05:49,058
Go wolves.

111
00:05:49,350 --> 00:05:50,350
What?

112
00:05:50,518 --> 00:05:55,940
You're a cheerleader, and by saying "go wolves," I'm initiating a conversation about something that interests you.

113
00:05:56,524 --> 00:05:59,568
Oh, are you one of those special ed kids?

114
00:05:59,819 --> 00:06:01,570
My mom says I'm special.

115
00:06:01,779 --> 00:06:03,280
Would you like to be friends?

116
00:06:03,656 --> 00:06:05,199
I don't think so.

117
00:06:05,449 --> 00:06:09,453
Are you sure? What if I told you I admired your boldly-applied makeup?

118
00:06:09,662 --> 00:06:11,288
J'ohh'

119
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:18,754
Greetings from stall number one.

120
00:06:18,963 --> 00:06:24,677
As much as I detest that odd-smelling cigarette, I do applaud your rule-breaking bravado.

121
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:28,556
My name is Sheldon. What's yours?

122
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:31,350
Hello, gentlemen.

123
00:06:32,309 --> 00:06:35,688
Did you watch any sports programs over the weekend?

124
00:06:36,897 --> 00:06:38,124
♪ that's what I like about...

125
00:06:38,148 --> 00:06:40,776
or perhaps did some kissing with girls?

126
00:06:42,903 --> 00:06:44,343
♪ that's what I like about you...

127
00:06:45,406 --> 00:06:46,532
can you hear me?

128
00:06:46,740 --> 00:06:47,740
♪ hey! Hey!

129
00:06:47,825 --> 00:06:50,619
♪ missy: 95, 96, 97. hey!

130
00:06:51,036 --> 00:06:52,036
98.

131
00:06:52,246 --> 00:06:54,832
99, 100.

132
00:06:55,082 --> 00:06:59,670
You missed 11 numbers. I don't need to be good at math. I have beautiful hair.

133
00:06:59,962 --> 00:07:02,006
That's the saddest thing I've ever heard.

134
00:07:02,882 --> 00:07:04,550
Didn't you already read that book?

135
00:07:05,134 --> 00:07:06,302
I'm reading it again.

136
00:07:06,510 --> 00:07:07,510
How come?

137
00:07:07,845 --> 00:07:08,929
I clearly missed something.

138
00:07:09,138 --> 00:07:12,349
I couldn't find one person who wanted to be my friend.

139
00:07:12,558 --> 00:07:14,268
Did you look at the card inside?

140
00:07:15,477 --> 00:07:16,103
Why?

141
00:07:16,312 --> 00:07:18,564
That's just other people who checked out the book.

142
00:07:18,981 --> 00:07:21,817
It's a list of losers like you who can't find a friend.

143
00:07:22,234 --> 00:07:23,234
So?

144
00:07:23,402 --> 00:07:25,821
So they're desperate, and you have their names.

145
00:07:27,072 --> 00:07:28,991
That's brilliant.

146
00:07:29,742 --> 00:07:30,826
Mm.

147
00:07:31,327 --> 00:07:33,454
Now, how is it that you can't count?

148
00:07:38,375 --> 00:07:41,170
♪ well, can't you see her standing there?

149
00:07:41,879 --> 00:07:44,173
so your father tells me you've been reading a new book?

150
00:07:44,381 --> 00:07:46,550
I am. It's about how to make friends.

151
00:07:47,551 --> 00:07:49,261
Is that something you're interested in?

152
00:07:49,470 --> 00:07:53,766
Not really, but missy told me it makes you sad that I don't have any.

153
00:07:54,642 --> 00:07:55,642
0h.

154
00:07:56,101 --> 00:07:58,145
I got to sew that girl's mouth shut.

155
00:07:59,146 --> 00:08:00,147
So how's it going?

156
00:08:00,356 --> 00:08:03,359
Well, the principles in the book didn't work for me at all.

157
00:08:03,734 --> 00:08:08,322
Although dad does like being called George over and over again, so you might give that a try.

158
00:08:08,906 --> 00:08:11,241
Baby, you don't have to go to any trouble for me.

159
00:08:11,575 --> 00:08:12,868
But I like doing things for you.

160
00:08:13,077 --> 00:08:14,244
You're a nice lady.

161
00:08:14,954 --> 00:08:17,039
But if the book's not working... It isn't.

162
00:08:17,790 --> 00:08:22,711
But I realized if I find the other people who checked out the book, they'd be looking for friends, too.

163
00:08:23,379 --> 00:08:25,005
That's really smart.

164
00:08:25,255 --> 00:08:27,466
Missy thought of it. You're kidding.

165
00:08:27,883 --> 00:08:29,885
I'm as surprised as you are.

166
00:08:30,219 --> 00:08:31,219
♪ oh, oh-oh, oh.

167
00:08:33,305 --> 00:08:35,182
Excuse me, Ms. Ingram?

168
00:08:35,474 --> 00:08:37,101
Sheldon, it's recess.

169
00:08:37,309 --> 00:08:39,019
Go outside, see what it's like.

170
00:08:39,228 --> 00:08:42,189
Are you by any chance Evelyn ingram?

171
00:08:42,815 --> 00:08:43,815
Yes.

172
00:08:43,941 --> 00:08:47,069
The same Evelyn ingram who checked out this book?

173
00:08:47,277 --> 00:08:51,532
And why is that your business? I just assumed you were looking to make a friend.

174
00:08:51,740 --> 00:08:52,950
Oh.

175
00:08:53,158 --> 00:08:54,618
Well, that was a long time ago.

176
00:08:54,827 --> 00:08:57,705
I had just gotten out of a very difficult relationship.

177
00:08:57,955 --> 00:08:59,373
With a friend? Friend.

178
00:08:59,581 --> 00:09:03,544
Ha! I gave that man three years of my life and my innocence.

179
00:09:03,752 --> 00:09:04,962
I don't know what that means.

180
00:09:05,170 --> 00:09:08,549
It means men are lying dogs only interested in one thing.

181
00:09:08,966 --> 00:09:10,718
Well, that's not the dictionary definition.

182
00:09:10,926 --> 00:09:11,552
Mm-hmm.

183
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:13,345
Always starts the same.

184
00:09:13,637 --> 00:09:19,268
A quick glance across the room, a casual hand on the shoulder, lunch at stuckey's.

185
00:09:19,476 --> 00:09:22,051
Oh, yeah, it's all romance and flowers in the beginning...

186
00:09:22,187 --> 00:09:24,857
The list proved to be a double-edged sword.

187
00:09:25,065 --> 00:09:30,779
It turns out self-help books written in 1936 were only of interest to adults.

188
00:09:30,988 --> 00:09:32,465
You're like, "I'm here because I care.

189
00:09:32,489 --> 00:09:34,449
And by the way, your phone could make calls, too."

190
00:09:34,575 --> 00:09:39,121
Then tries to turn this whole thing around like it's all about you being needy, and you're like, "yes, I have needs!

191
00:09:39,329 --> 00:09:40,622
That's what makes me human!"

192
00:09:40,998 --> 00:09:43,333
Emotionally troubled adults.

193
00:09:43,542 --> 00:09:45,002
Child, go outside.

194
00:09:48,422 --> 00:09:50,424
Yes, that's my signature. Why do you ask?

195
00:09:50,632 --> 00:09:52,968
I'm seeking people who want to make friends.

196
00:09:53,343 --> 00:09:54,845
I wasn't interested in friends.

197
00:09:55,054 --> 00:09:57,306
I took out that book to help me become school principal.

198
00:09:57,514 --> 00:10:01,226
Did it work? Are we sitting in the principal's office?

199
00:10:01,435 --> 00:10:03,145
No. And you know why?

200
00:10:03,604 --> 00:10:06,899
Because Victoria macelroy is not a man!

201
00:10:07,733 --> 00:10:10,444
Soto be clear, you're not interested in making friends?

202
00:10:10,652 --> 00:10:18,077
What I'm interested in is in living long enough to see women no longer treated like second-class citizens.

203
00:10:18,285 --> 00:10:23,082
Well if your goal is along life, that chili cheeseburger is a step in the wrong direction.

204
00:10:23,999 --> 00:10:26,085
Food is all I have.

205
00:10:26,919 --> 00:10:30,881
Like isaid, emotionally troubled adults.

206
00:10:31,090 --> 00:10:32,299
You gonna watch me eat?

207
00:10:32,508 --> 00:10:33,508
Get out of here.

208
00:10:36,887 --> 00:10:42,184
Everybody told me, "Hubert, don't date someone from work.“ but did I listen? No.

209
00:10:42,392 --> 00:10:45,145
Let me tell you something. She likes to say I stole her innocence?

210
00:10:45,354 --> 00:10:46,563
Well, what about my innocence?

211
00:10:46,772 --> 00:10:48,291
I was never the same after we broke up.

212
00:10:48,315 --> 00:10:54,822
Two years later, Mr. Givens became the high school principal, which might be why Ms. Macelroy became morbidly obese.

213
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:59,368
I had the brains, I had the charm, and I don't like to brag, but I was pretty easy on the eyes back then.

214
00:10:59,576 --> 00:11:01,620
That woman got herself one heck of a package.

215
00:11:01,829 --> 00:11:03,705
But some people don't want to be satisfied.

216
00:11:05,958 --> 00:11:07,334
Is that any good?

217
00:11:08,127 --> 00:11:09,127
I'm afraid not.

218
00:11:09,169 --> 00:11:10,921
I failed to make a single friend.

219
00:11:11,130 --> 00:11:13,966
That sucks. I have a tough time making friends, too.

220
00:11:14,174 --> 00:11:16,468
The worst part is, it was important to my mom.

221
00:11:16,718 --> 00:11:19,471
My parents pressure me about making friends all the time.

222
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:21,640
So you understand what I'm going through.

223
00:11:23,058 --> 00:11:25,352
Oh, I read that book. It's excellent.

224
00:11:25,853 --> 00:11:26,979
Are you into rocketry?

225
00:11:27,187 --> 00:11:33,735
I started with water propulsion, worked my way up to solid fuel, then went back to water after I set our garage on fire.

226
00:11:34,194 --> 00:11:40,033
Nice. I also tried to get some uranium and build an atomic engine, but that stuff's hard to find.

227
00:11:41,285 --> 00:11:42,786
Probably for the best.

228
00:11:42,995 --> 00:11:45,080
Well, good luck on your quest to find a friend.

229
00:11:45,455 --> 00:11:46,455
You, too.

230
00:11:52,838 --> 00:11:55,174
If you haven't found one yet, I have good news.

231
00:11:58,468 --> 00:12:01,096
Mom, what do you know about the drug Prozac?

232
00:12:01,638 --> 00:12:02,638
Not much.

233
00:12:02,681 --> 00:12:04,808
Supposed to make people happier. Why?

234
00:12:05,767 --> 00:12:08,812
I was just thinking some of my teachers might benefit from it.

235
00:12:10,439 --> 00:12:13,400
Speaking of which, you'll be happy to know I made a friend today.

236
00:12:14,193 --> 00:12:15,193
0h.

237
00:12:16,361 --> 00:12:17,738
Well, that's nice.

238
00:12:17,946 --> 00:12:19,865
But, as I said, this isn't for me.

239
00:12:20,073 --> 00:12:21,200
It's for you.

240
00:12:22,409 --> 00:12:23,577
He made a friend!

241
00:12:23,827 --> 00:12:24,827
He did it!

242
00:12:24,912 --> 00:12:27,664
Our little boy has got a friend.

243
00:12:27,873 --> 00:12:29,458
Who is it? Who cares?

244
00:12:29,666 --> 00:12:31,752
It's a human being. It's a warm body.

245
00:12:32,419 --> 00:12:33,670
Mom?

246
00:12:33,879 --> 00:12:34,463
Yes, honey?

247
00:12:34,671 --> 00:12:36,256
Can I come in? Of course.

248
00:12:36,465 --> 00:12:37,883
Be cool.

249
00:12:38,967 --> 00:12:43,180
What's up, sweetie? I was wondering when I could continue my rocketry hobby.

250
00:12:43,513 --> 00:12:45,390
Uh, Sheldon, we've been through this.

251
00:12:45,599 --> 00:12:47,202
You can build them. You just can't launch them.

252
00:12:47,226 --> 00:12:49,102
But launching them is the fun part.

253
00:12:49,394 --> 00:12:52,689
Tell that to the family of squirrels who died in the fire. Okay.

254
00:12:52,898 --> 00:12:55,359
It was just something I was hoping to do with my new friend.

255
00:12:55,776 --> 00:12:56,568
Hang on.

256
00:12:56,777 --> 00:12:58,028
Uh, yes.

257
00:12:58,403 --> 00:12:59,403
It's fine.

258
00:12:59,446 --> 00:13:00,239
Ban is lifted.

259
00:13:00,447 --> 00:13:02,157
Mary... It's for his friend.

260
00:13:02,366 --> 00:13:03,533
He's got a friend.

261
00:13:04,368 --> 00:13:05,768
Besides, those could have been rats.

262
00:13:05,911 --> 00:13:07,663
Their bodies were so badly burned.

263
00:13:08,455 --> 00:13:10,707
I thought we were being cool, but okay.

264
00:13:11,416 --> 00:13:12,459
Hey, here's an idea.

265
00:13:12,668 --> 00:13:14,920
Why don't you invite your new buddy over for dinner?

266
00:13:15,170 --> 00:13:19,758
Why? Well, honey, um, that is what friends do.

267
00:13:19,967 --> 00:13:23,679
My mother asked me to ask you if you'd like to join us for dinner.

268
00:13:24,054 --> 00:13:26,974
Why? I was hoping you'd know.

269
00:13:33,313 --> 00:13:34,313
He's here!

270
00:13:34,439 --> 00:13:39,382
Everybody, stay calm! Just a normal day, just a normal dinner. Can I eat in front of the TV?

271
00:13:39,528 --> 00:13:41,528
No, you can eat in front of Sheldon's friend.

272
00:13:41,655 --> 00:13:45,784
Aw, fudge. I know what "fudge" means, and you are right on the edge, young lady.

273
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:47,828
Welcome.

274
00:13:58,046 --> 00:13:59,047
George sr.: 80, uh...

275
00:13:59,298 --> 00:14:01,174
Tam. What kind of name is that?

276
00:14:01,550 --> 00:14:03,010
Vietnamese. Sir.

277
00:14:03,218 --> 00:14:05,138
Sure. You know, I spent a little time over there.

278
00:14:05,429 --> 00:14:06,430
Army.

279
00:14:09,599 --> 00:14:11,226
Your mom's name isn't Kim-Lee, is it?

280
00:14:11,518 --> 00:14:12,811
No. Sir.

281
00:14:13,270 --> 00:14:14,270
Good.

282
00:14:14,438 --> 00:14:16,898
I mean, you know, it's a small country.

283
00:14:18,150 --> 00:14:18,942
So...

284
00:14:19,151 --> 00:14:20,402
Mary, how's that food coming?

285
00:14:20,610 --> 00:14:21,610
Almost.

286
00:14:21,737 --> 00:14:23,572
So, Vietnam, like in Rambo.

287
00:14:23,947 --> 00:14:25,699
Yes. That's a cool movie.

288
00:14:26,033 --> 00:14:27,033
Yes.

289
00:14:27,075 --> 00:14:28,075
Are you in it?

290
00:14:28,493 --> 00:14:29,911
-Hmm.

291
00:14:31,038 --> 00:14:32,956
Why would you think you knew his mom?

292
00:14:33,457 --> 00:14:34,750
All right, tam.

293
00:14:34,958 --> 00:14:37,711
I decided I was gonna make you a real Texas dinner.

294
00:14:38,045 --> 00:14:39,379
Barbecued chicken and brisket.

295
00:14:39,588 --> 00:14:40,380
Thank you.

296
00:14:40,589 --> 00:14:44,009
Well, I figured you were probably tired of stuff wiggling around on your plate.

297
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:46,136
Okay, let's say grace.

298
00:14:46,345 --> 00:14:49,848
Now, tam, when I say "Jesus," feel free to say the word "Buddha" in your head.

299
00:14:50,057 --> 00:14:51,058
I'm actually catholic.

300
00:14:51,308 --> 00:14:53,935
Oh. Well, that's too bad.

301
00:14:54,519 --> 00:14:57,332
Thank you, god, for this food, and bless the hands that prepared it.

302
00:14:57,356 --> 00:15:00,359
And thank you so much for Sheldon's new friend.

303
00:15:00,567 --> 00:15:01,985
Amen.

304
00:15:03,403 --> 00:15:05,238
So, tam, tell us about your family.

305
00:15:05,447 --> 00:15:06,865
What brings y'all to Texas?

306
00:15:07,074 --> 00:15:08,950
Well, after the American war...

307
00:15:09,159 --> 00:15:10,285
You mean the Vietnam war.

308
00:15:10,494 --> 00:15:11,828
We call it the American war.

309
00:15:12,371 --> 00:15:17,810
Hmm. Anyway, after the war, my father was sent to a reeducation camp because he fought on the wrong side.

310
00:15:17,834 --> 00:15:18,834
You mean our side.

311
00:15:19,711 --> 00:15:21,296
I was trying to be nice.

312
00:15:21,630 --> 00:15:28,428
So, for many years, my mother and my sisters and I were very poor and very often didn't have much to eat.

313
00:15:28,637 --> 00:15:30,514
Then, when he was released...

314
00:15:30,722 --> 00:15:32,557
What'd they teach him at the reeducation camp?

315
00:15:32,766 --> 00:15:33,850
How to be a communist.

316
00:15:34,059 --> 00:15:35,519
Cool, like in Rambo.

317
00:15:37,396 --> 00:15:45,445
When he was released, we escaped on a small boat and spent many weeks at sea dodging Cambodian pirates until we reached Thailand.

318
00:15:45,862 --> 00:15:48,407
Oh, that's supposed to be a beautiful country.

319
00:15:48,782 --> 00:15:50,742
Lovely beaches. I wouldn't know.

320
00:15:50,992 --> 00:15:56,123
We were forced to live in a refugee camp, where the only thing we had to eat were pigeons and rats.

321
00:15:56,373 --> 00:15:58,583
Ha! That's a job for hot sauce.

322
00:16:00,585 --> 00:16:05,132
Finally, we were allowed to come to United States and start over in galveston.

323
00:16:05,340 --> 00:16:07,926
My father saved money and bought his own shrimp boat.

324
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:10,846
There's a happy ending. Huh?

325
00:16:11,054 --> 00:16:15,058
See, kids? When the going gets tough, america provides. It did.

326
00:16:15,267 --> 00:16:19,646
Until the ku klux klan burned our boat and chased us away.

327
00:16:20,522 --> 00:16:22,107
You gonna put a good spin on that one?

328
00:16:22,607 --> 00:16:25,861
So we came to medford and opened up a convenience store.

329
00:16:26,069 --> 00:16:32,451
My parents work 16 hours a day, seven days a week for very little money.

330
00:16:38,123 --> 00:16:39,916
Well, that was depressing.

331
00:16:46,590 --> 00:16:48,800
I don't understand why it didn't launch.

332
00:16:49,009 --> 00:16:50,010
Me, neither.

333
00:16:50,218 --> 00:16:53,263
Evil Nazi scientists did it 50 years ago.

334
00:16:53,889 --> 00:16:54,931
How hard could it be?

335
00:17:00,187 --> 00:17:01,187
Can I help you?

336
00:17:01,354 --> 00:17:03,565
Sorry to bother you, ma'am. FBI.

337
00:17:03,773 --> 00:17:05,650
We're looking for a Sheldon Lee Cooper.

338
00:17:07,068 --> 00:17:07,819
George!

339
00:17:08,028 --> 00:17:09,613
George sr.: I'm on the can.

340
00:17:09,863 --> 00:17:11,281
Now!

341
00:17:12,949 --> 00:17:16,912
What? These gentlemen are with the FBI. They want to talk to Sheldon.

342
00:17:17,913 --> 00:17:18,913
What?

343
00:17:18,997 --> 00:17:19,997
We... uh...

344
00:17:20,123 --> 00:17:22,125
Y-You fellas must have made a mistake.

345
00:17:22,334 --> 00:17:23,334
Sheldon's nine.

346
00:17:23,460 --> 00:17:28,381
Well, someone living at this address recently called a mining operation in Canada and tried to buy uranium.

347
00:17:28,924 --> 00:17:30,383
Okay, maybe it's not a mistake.

348
00:17:30,592 --> 00:17:32,010
It's ridiculous.

349
00:17:32,427 --> 00:17:33,467
Let's just go talk to him.

350
00:17:33,553 --> 00:17:34,137
Huh?

351
00:17:34,346 --> 00:17:36,181
He's just a little, itty-bitty thing.

352
00:17:36,389 --> 00:17:37,557
Really, he's harmless.

353
00:17:42,062 --> 00:17:43,605
Sorry!

354
00:17:44,439 --> 00:17:45,690
He's in the garage.
